From 25a7d99f26b95988513cbce3d6c13323cb577985 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:58:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: frames with GUI elements over the picture step
 down where they do not keep up

The automatic fidelity step-down (CDVStepDown) measures the frames
without GUI elements only, and draws every frame of a format at the
level it finds for them. A frame with GUI elements over the picture (the
OSD, the player information, a window over the dimmed video) is composed
with them and takes longer: on a small GPU those frames miss their
refreshes while the others keep up, and nothing measured them. Reported
on LibreELEC's forum with a Ryzen 5 3500U (Vega 8) and a profile 7 film
with its full enhancement layer: frames dropped while the OSD was open.
Only the frames with GUI elements of a profile 5 picture had levels of
their own (CDVGuiLevel, the conversion of the pixels drawn over).

CDVGuiStep measures the frames with GUI elements whose ladder has a
level below that of the frames without them, as CDVGuiLevel measures
its own, and steps them down that ladder by the rule of CDVStepDown,
from that level: two windows behind in a row (or one in which the GPU
cannot keep up at all), by both the GPU times and the missed refreshes;
never to a level that is measured to save nothing; nothing while frame
ahead can still be had; back up when the level above would fit with
room to spare, a minute of film after it went down, the wait doubling
where that level does not keep up after all. With the composer of ETSI
GS CCM 001 in the source transport those are the levels that resample
the enhancement layer from two samples (1 and 2), measured against the
composer of the clause on 14 pictures of a profile 7 FEL stream: at
level 2, 15.6 % of the luma samples and 5.0 to 6.0 % of the chroma
samples another code, at most 15 codes of 4095.

Only while GUI elements are over the picture, and only where those
frames are measured not to keep up: the frames without GUI elements are
drawn at their level as before, and so are the frames with GUI elements
on a GPU where they keep up (the same bytes as before). The two
decisions never share a frame: one that CDVGuiLevel measures is not
measured here. The Dolby Vision page goes on giving the level of the
frames without GUI elements, as it gives their way of drawing. What is
found is kept from one video to the next, not from one session to the
next. Every change is logged with the window that decided it and what
each level took.
---
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiStep.h                | 508 ++++++++++++++++++
 .../windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp | 122 ++++-
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.h  |  24 +-
 3 files changed, 643 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiStep.h

diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiStep.h b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiStep.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..386a5b0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/DVGuiStep.h
@@ -0,0 +1,508 @@
+/*
